<% content_for(:page_title) { t('preservica.about.title') } %>

<div class="col-lg-9 py-3">
  <h1 class="mb-4"><%= t('preservica.about.title') %></h1>

  <%# Text from the Universal Access portal's About page (uni.access.preservica.com/portal/en-US/about-us). %>
  <p class="lead">Rod Library’s digital collections include material that is either born-digital or digitized according to prevailing standards, described using metadata standards to facilitate discovery, preserved in recommended formats to enable long-term accessibility, and, finally, published online for teaching, learning, and research.</p>
  <p>Digital collections encompass a wide variety of formats, including photographs, print materials, moving images, audio recordings, artworks, objects, and much more. Our digital collections are created to support student success, facilitate learning and research, advance information literacy, and increase access to rare or fragile items that may be too delicate to be handled.</p>
  <p>Rod Library offers public access to a wide range of information, including rare books, manuscripts, and institutional records in Special Collections &amp; University Archives; ethnographic materials in the UNI Museum; and creative and scholarly works in Digital Scholarship. For more information about our collections or units, please visit the <%= link_to 'Distinctive Collections and Digital Strategies LibGuide', 'https://guides.lib.uni.edu/dcds' %>.</p>
  <div class="border-start border-4 border-warning bg-body-tertiary px-3 py-2 mb-4">
    Materials in Rod Library’s Digital Collections should be evaluated in the context of the period in which they were created. Rod Library does not endorse views expressed in such materials.
  </div>

  <h2 class="h3 mt-4"><%= t('preservica.about.whats_here') %></h2>
  <p><%= link_to t('preservica.about.browse_collections'), search_catalog_path(f: { level: ['top'] }, sort: 'title') %></p>

  <h2 class="h3 mt-4"><%= t('preservica.about.viewing_heading') %></h2>
  <p><%= t('preservica.about.viewing_text') %></p>

  <%# TODO (library): add "Using the collections" (permissions and reproduction policy). %>

  <h2 class="h3 mt-4"><%= t('preservica.about.technology_heading') %></h2>
  <p>Digital Collections is built from open standards and open-source software, most of it shared and maintained by libraries and archives around the world.</p>
  <dl>
    <dt class="mt-3"><%= link_to 'Preservica', 'https://preservica.com/' %></dt>
    <dd class="mb-0">The library’s digital preservation system and the home of every item. It stores the original files, checks them regularly so they stay intact, and keeps them readable as file formats change. Its public portal, Universal Access, is where you view and download items.</dd>
    <dt class="mt-3"><%= link_to 'Blacklight', 'https://projectblacklight.org/' %></dt>
    <dd class="mb-0">Open-source discovery software used by many university libraries. It provides this site’s search box, filters, collection pages, item pages and map. It is built with Ruby on Rails.</dd>
    <dt class="mt-3"><%= link_to 'Apache Solr', 'https://solr.apache.org/' %></dt>
    <dd class="mb-0">An open-source search engine. It holds an index of the descriptions of public items, so searches, filters and sorting are fast. It holds descriptions only, not the files themselves.</dd>
    <dt class="mt-3">Harvester</dt>
    <dd class="mb-0">A small Python program written for Rod Library. Each night it copies the descriptions of public items from Preservica into Solr, along with the folder structure and thumbnail images. Items that are restricted or withdrawn in Preservica are removed from this site.</dd>
    <dt class="mt-3"><%= link_to 'MODS and Dublin Core', 'https://www.loc.gov/standards/mods/' %></dt>
    <dd class="mb-0">Metadata standards for describing items: titles, names, dates, subjects, places and rights. They supply the filters on the search page, such as Name, Genre, Subject and Date.</dd>
    <dt class="mt-3"><%= link_to 'IIIF and serverless-iiif', 'https://iiif.io/' %></dt>
    <dd class="mb-0">The International Image Interoperability Framework is a shared standard for delivering images online. We are introducing it, with Samvera’s serverless-iiif running on Amazon Web Services, so images can open in a zoomable viewer here and be used in other IIIF tools.</dd>
    <dt class="mt-3"><%= link_to 'Leaflet, OpenStreetMap and Allmaps', 'https://leafletjs.com/' %></dt>
    <dd class="mb-0">Leaflet draws the maps, using OpenStreetMap’s street map. Photographs with locations appear as points on the Map page. Allmaps lays georeferenced historical maps over the modern map.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 class="h3 mt-4"><%= t('preservica.about.more_heading') %></h2>
  <p><%= t('preservica.about.more_text') %> <%= link_to 'Distinctive Collections and Digital Strategies LibGuide', 'https://guides.lib.uni.edu/dcds' %>.</p>

  <h2 class="h3 mt-4"><%= t('preservica.about.contact_heading') %></h2>
  <p><%= t('preservica.about.contact_text') %> <%= mail_to 'library@uni.edu' %>.</p>
</div>
